<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Issa (clan)</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Issa Somali</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Issa are a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalis">Somali</a> clan of the Dir family, living in Djibouti, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somali-region">Somali Region</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> and north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalia">Somalia</a>. In the 1880s French officers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonce-lagarde">Léonce Lagarde</a> signed protection treaties with Issa clans, and their coast became part of French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-somaliland">Somaliland</a>. Rivalry between the Issa and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afar">Afar</a> shaped the colony's politics, and Djibouti's first president, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hassan-gouled-aptidon">Hassan Gouled Aptidon</a>, was an Issa.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/issa-clan.jpg" alt="Issa (clan)" width="320" height="179" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Skilla1st · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sheikh_Issa_Tomb.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
